  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nThe main forecast concern this forecast period will be the well \nabove normal temperatures that being to build into the area today \nand are forecast to continue through next week. These above normal \ntemperatures will come courtesy of a upper level ridge which is \ncentered over the southwestern CONUS and southern Rockies this \nmorning. Today this feature will build eastward into the southern \nand central Plains providing height rises across the region. This \ncoupled with modest WAA will drive highs into the upper 80s to mid \n90s. Tonight, a upper level shortwave will round the ridge and move \ninto northern Missouri as a 45-50kt southwesterly LLJ noses into the \narea. Despite lacking moisture, a few showers or thunderstorms (15-\n25%) will be possible across northern Missouri overnight into \ntomorrow morning. Tomorrow the upper level ridge axis builds \ndirectly over the region. This coupled with strong WAA will push \ntemperatures 10-15 degrees above normal with highs in the mid 90s to \nnear 100. The upper level ridge will remain in control over the area \nthrough Tuesday with highs remaining in the mid 90s to near 100. \nFortunately, dewpoints in the 60s Sunday through Tuesday will keep \nheat index values from being much warmer than the air temperatures \nwith heat indicies ranging between 95-104.\n\nTuesday night into Wednesday, a upper level shortwave trough will \nmove from the central Rockies into the central Plains and into the \nUpper Midwest. This will cause the ridge to flatten over the region \nas the ridge axis shifts into the southeastern CONUS. This will push \na cold front towards the area Wednesday however highs will still \nremain in the mid 90s to near 100. However, better moisture may pool \nout ahead of this front driving heat index values into the 100-105 \nrange. Late Wednesday night into Thursday morning, the front may \nreach northern Missouri with a nocturnal LLJ focused on that area. \nThis may allow a few showers and thunderstorms to develop across \nnortheastern Kansas and northern Missouri (15-25%). Despite the \nchance for morning storms, temperatures are still forecast to reach \nthe mid to upper 90s again on Thursday. By the end of the week, the \nlocal area will be on the western periphery of the upper level ridge \nover the southeastern CONUS which may allow shower and thunderstorm \nchances to continue Thursday night into Friday (15-25%).",
